`ecq-value-materialization@1.0.0`,\n- Corrado items 7–9,\n- `valueSurface`,\n- canonical transform seams,\n- shell opacity,\n- registration zero,\n- envelope versioning,\n\nhave no causal requirement from the MLB desktop cleanup or the 09-24 slate.\n\nThe chat nevertheless adopted them as if they were part of the same session.\n\n### Bleed 3: DevPad clipboard entered too\n\nThe DevPad D-2 right-click/copy behavior is completely orthogonal to MLB methodology.\n\nIts presence demonstrates that the chat had stopped operating as a bounded session and became a general-purpose GCE return interpreter.\n\nThat is strong evidence of frame failure.\n\n---\n\n# H. SLICE AUDIT\n\n### 1. MLB estate recon\n**Why:** understand old versions and cleanup safety.  \n**Artifact:** 11 recon deliverables, manifest, cleanup decision register.  \n**Necessary:** yes.  \n**Could be absorbed:** already coherent.\n\n### 2. Falcon pregame freeze\n**Why:** produce fresh point-in-time data.  \n**Artifact:** frozen 09-24 package.  \n**Necessary:** yes.  \n**Could be absorbed:** yes, and it was.\n\n### 3. Experimental engine run\n**Why:** analyze the slate.  \n**Artifact:** full grid/replay.  \n**Necessary:** yes.  \n**Could be absorbed:** yes.\n\n### 4. Kimi corrective slice\n**Why:** fix real state/logic defects.  \n**Artifact:** r7 corrective baseline.  \n**Necessary:** mostly yes.  \n**Could be absorbed:** some issues could have been fixed in one coherent build pass rather than treated as several conceptual sub-gates.\n\n### 5. Luce verification of r7\n**Why:** independently confirm defects/fixes.  \n**Artifact:** VERIFIED WITH DEBT return.  \n**Necessary:** yes.  \n**Could be absorbed:** no; independent verification was useful.\n\n### 6. Totals calibration research gate\n**Why:** understand μ compression.  \n**Artifact:** 14-game research corpus analysis.  \n**Necessary:** useful, but not necessary to finish the 09-24 live session.  \n**Could be deferred:** yes.\n\n### 7. Scorecard rerun request after totals research\n**Why:** rematerialize visible scorecard after no math change.  \n**Artifact:** apparently not completed because credits expired.  \n**Necessary:** low.  \n**Could be absorbed:** yes; prior verified replay already existed.\n\n### 8. Freeze after Kimi credits exhausted\n**Why:** natural stop.  \n**Artifact:** accepted freeze state.  \n**Necessary:** yes.  \n**Should have ended session:** yes.\n\n### 9. ECQ FIX 1\n**Why:** unrelated identity defect.  \n**Artifact:** assertion-content-bound identity.  \n**Necessary to ECQ:** yes.  \n**Necessary to this session:** no.\n\n### 10. Luce FIX 1 re-verification\n**Why:** verify identity repair.  \n**Artifact:** verification pathway.  \n**Necessary to ECQ:** yes.  \n**Necessary here:** no.\n\n### 11. Chiave seam/version gate\n**Why:** envelope/seam policy.  \n**Artifact:** architecture-gate verdict.  \n**Necessary to ECQ:** perhaps.  \n**Necessary here:** no.\n\n### 12. Nico seam-completion build\n**Why:** canonical transform + shell `valueSurface`.  \n**Artifact:** new files, 178 tests.  \n**Necessary to ECQ:** yes.  \n**Necessary here:** no.\n\n### 13. Luce seam verification prompt\n**Why:** certify seam completion.  \n**Artifact:** prompt only in this transcript segment.  \n**Necessary to ECQ:** yes.  \n**Necessary here:** no.\n\n### 14. DevPad D-2 verification\n**Why:** validate clipboard behavior.  \n**Artifact:** static verification return.  \n**Necessary to DevPad:** yes.  \n**Necessary here:** no.\n\n---\n\n# I. VERIFICATION AUDIT\n\n## Legitimate defects\n\nThese were real and worth catching:\n\n- odds-input consumption uncertainty,\n- run-line non-cover probability construction,\n- juice-gate dual accounting,\n- `total`/`totals` schema drift,\n- assertion IDs being hash-bound without assertion content,\n- missing canonical transform/valueSurface seam,\n- DevPad lacking actual behavioral certification.\n\n## Useful evidence checks\n\n- frozen file hashes,\n- BetMGM perturbation,\n- fail-closed MIL@PHI behavior,\n- state-count reconciliation,\n- materialization hash mutation probes,\n- registration staying zero,\n- static renderer hash confirmation.\n\n## Low-value nook-and-cranny findings\n\n- repeated recounting of already-established r7 totals,\n- rematerializing a scorecard when no math changed,\n- repeatedly restating that totals remained conflict after no recalibration,\n- repeatedly restating registration=0 across several ECQ handoffs,\n- repeatedly reasserting no clock reads / one hash primitive after already verified.\n\nThese are not wrong, but they are where verification began replacing progress.\n\n## Findings that triggered architectural reconsideration\n\nThe most consequential example:\n\n- a real materialization identity defect led not only to the direct hash fix, but then into envelope-version discussion, canonical transform work, shell seam work, registration posture, and another full verification chain.\n\nThose may all be valid ECQ issues, but they were **architecturally broader than the original defect**.\n\n## Where verification became recursion\n\nLuce itself mostly behaved correctly: it found real defects and reported bounded evidence.\n\nThe recursion came from orchestration:\n\n> Luce finding → Coda reframing → new Nico slice → new Luce verification → Chiave gate → new Nico seam slice → new Luce verification.\n\nThe verifier was not the root cause. The orchestration repeatedly converted findings into broader gates.\n\n---\n\n# J. VISIBLE OUTCOME TEST\n\n### After Falcon freeze\nDon had a visible usable thing: **today’s frozen slate package**.\n\n### After the experimental engine run\nDon had a visible usable thing: **full 12-game scorecard / zero-candidate outcome**.\n\n### After Kimi r7\nDon had a visible usable thing: **corrected engine state with four legitimate reviews and no candidates**.\n\n### After Luce r7 verification\nDon had a visible usable thing: **independently verified accepted experimental baseline**.\n\nAt this point the user-facing outcome was already strong.\n\n### During totals calibration research\nVisible outcome became weaker. The work was useful, but there was no new runnable card or system behavior.\n\n### During ECQ identity/seam work\nFor this chat’s original purpose, Don had **no new visible MLB outcome at all**. Hours of process could accumulate while the original task was already done.\n\n### During DevPad static verification\nAgain, no MLB outcome. Even within DevPad, final behavioral validation was still pending.\n\nThis is exactly where process overtook runnable progress.\n\n---\n\n# K. SESSION FRAME HEALTH\n\n### Real session header?\n**No strong persistent header governed the whole chat.**\n\nThe chat had local labels like “MLB Experimental Lane,” but no durable session-level frame that prevented unrelated project work from entering.\n\n### Task explicitly bounded?\nInitially, partially. The MLB cleanup and Falcon prompts were well bounded.\n\nLater, no. The chat accepted ECQ and DevPad tasks without re-framing.\n\n### Authority clear?\nMostly yes. Don remained final authority, and seat roles were generally understood.\n\n### Expected artifact clear?\nEarly: yes.  \nLater: increasingly no.\n\nExamples:\n\n- after r7 verification, was the desired endpoint “accepted baseline,” “new scorecard,” “totals model,” or “full methodology corpus”?\n- after ECQ FIX 1, was the endpoint hash correctness, envelope policy, full seam closure, or registration readiness?\n\nThe expected artifact kept expanding.\n\n### Stop condition?\nWeak.\n\nThe strongest stop condition appeared accidentally when Kimi credits ran out.\n\n### Session footer / outcome ledger?\nNot consistently.\n\nThere were many local statuses, but no single session footer that said:\n\n> Original ask complete / these items deferred / do not continue in this thread.\n\n### When controls disappeared\nThe controls began degrading after the first successful engine run and were effectively gone once ECQ and DevPad work entered the same thread.\n\n---\n\n# L. WHAT SHOULD HAVE HAPPENED\n\nFor the MLB task, the shortest sane path was:\n\n**MLB cleanup recon**  \n→ review known risks  \n→ **Falcon frozen pregame package**  \n→ **one engine run**  \n→ inspect scorecard  \n→ fix only proven blocking implementation defects  \n→ **one independent verification pass**  \n→ freeze accepted experimental baseline  \n→ close session.\n\nTotals calibration should have been opened as a **separate research session** because it required:\n\n- a corpus,\n- morning-after grading,\n- future scored games,\n- out-of-sample evaluation.\n\nECQ materialization work should have lived in its own thread.\n\nDevPad clipboard work should have lived in its own thread.\n\nThe key correction is not “fewer tests.” It is:\n\n> **testing inside coherent passes, rather than turning every finding into a new conversational operating frame.**\n\n---\n\n# M. SALVAGE\n\n## KEEP\n\n- MLB estate recon.\n- Falcon 09-24 frozen package.\n- Experimental engine full-grid run.\n- odds-consumption perturbation evidence.\n- r7 run-line fail-closed behavior.\n- juice-gate single-state fix.\n- `total` ingestion normalization.\n- Luce r7 verification.\n- totals compression diagnosis.\n- 14-game corpus inventory.\n- ECQ assertion-content hash fix.\n- ECQ seam implementation.\n- DevPad served-build/static verification evidence.\n\n## VERIFY\n\n- totals methodology scaling conclusions beyond the diagnosed compression.\n- any claim that F1 should be exactly 5–6× larger.\n- any park-factor conversion proposed from the tiny corpus.\n- ECQ selection-target codec end-to-end behavior.\n- DevPad right-click behavior under real operator interaction.\n\n## DISCARD\n\nAs session artifacts, not code:\n\n- repeated “next gate” prompts after a verified accepted baseline already existed.\n- the extra 09-24 scorecard rerun request when no math changed.\n- repeated certification prose restating already-established invariants.\n- unrelated prompts embedded in this MLB thread.\n\n## UNKNOWN\n\n- whether all sandbox-only regression artifacts still exist.\n- whether every ECQ handoff mentioned was ultimately verified.\n- whether the DevPad live interaction matrix was ever executed.\n- whether any later scorecard was actually rematerialized after the Kimi credit exhaustion point.\n\n---\n\n# N. ROOT-CAUSE CANDIDATES\n\n### 1. **Missing session framing — HIGH evidence**\n\nStrongest factor.\n\nThe session did not maintain a durable “what are we doing / what closes this session” frame.\n\n### 2. **Project/context overloading — HIGH evidence**\n\nDirectly demonstrated by ECQ and DevPad work entering an MLB experimental chat.\n\n### 3. **Coda orchestration drift — HIGH evidence**\n\nCoda repeatedly turned returned findings into new slices and verification loops instead of closing completed work.\n\n### 4. **Overslicing — HIGH evidence**\n\nEspecially after r7 verification and within ECQ seam work.\n\n### 5. **Unclear stop condition — HIGH evidence**\n\nThe session had many local PASS/PROCEED statuses but no global “done.”\n\n### 6. **Verifier recursion — MEDIUM-HIGH evidence**\n\nVerification was usually valid, but its outputs repeatedly became inputs to fresh architecture/build cycles.\n\n### 7. **Handoff information loss — MEDIUM evidence**\n\nThe repeated need to restate assumptions and ask for rematerialized scorecards suggests partial state fragmentation across seats.\n\n### 8. **Builder interruption — MEDIUM evidence**\n\nLess obvious in the MLB path, but stronger in the generalized GCE pattern where builders were repeatedly sent back for narrowly re-scoped fixes before one final user-visible closure.\n\n### 9. **Another cause: success failed to terminate the session — HIGH evidence**\n\nThe session repeatedly achieved a satisfactory bounded result and then continued because another adjacent issue was available.\n\nThat may be the most concise behavioral description of the loop.\n\n---\n\n# O. EARLY WARNING SIGNALS\n\nThe first signals that should have triggered a circuit breaker were:\n\n1. **The 09-24 engine had already produced a complete frozen slate and zero-candidate result, but the session immediately pivoted into methodology development instead of closing the live run.**\n\n2. **After Luce VERIFIED WITH DEBT, the accepted r7 baseline still did not terminate the session.** A verified baseline should have been a natural footer.\n\n3. **A new scorecard rerun was requested even though no totals math changed.** This was repeated certification without a materially new runnable system state.\n\n4. **ECQ materialization appeared inside an MLB experimental thread.** That should have triggered an immediate “new session / wrong frame” boundary.\n\n5. **DevPad clipboard verification then appeared too.** At that point the absence of a session header/footer was no longer incidental; it was operationally consequential.\n\nThe disappearing header/footer correlates strongly with the drift.\n\n---\n\n# P. FINAL POSTMORTEM\n\n**SESSION STATUS:**  \n**LOOP-CONTAMINATED**\n\n**ORIGINAL ASK:**  \nReconcile and clean up the old MLB experimental data system, then use the corrected experimental stack to run and inspect the 2026-09-24 MLB slate.\n\n**ACTUAL DELIVERABLE:**  \nA verified MLB frozen-data/engine baseline with zero valid candidates and several real implementation/methodology defects isolated and corrected, plus substantial unrelated ECQ and DevPad work that entered the same chat.\n\n**LOOP STARTED:**  \nImmediately after the successful 2026-09-24 engine run, when the session shifted from completing the live MLB task into a new Kimi corrective/methodology program without first closing the original session; it became unmistakable when ECQ materialization work entered the same thread.\n\n**PRIMARY FAILURE MECHANISM:**  \nSuccessful intermediate results did not trigger closure; instead, each newly surfaced issue was converted into another seat/slice/verification cycle, while unrelated project work entered an already-unbounded chat.\n\n**TIME / COMPLEXITY INFLATION:**  \nMaterial. A bounded cleanup + live experimental run expanded into methodology research, recursive verification, architecture/seam work, and unrelated DevPad validation. The complexity grew several multiples beyond the original operational task.\n\n**SALVAGEABLE STATE:**  \nHigh. Most technical outputs appear useful: the MLB frozen slate, r7 corrections, Luce verification, totals diagnosis, ECQ identity fix, and seam work are salvageable. The primary waste is orchestration/process overhead and cross-project thread contamination, not necessarily the underlying code.\n\n**FIRST CIRCUIT BREAKER THAT SHOULD HAVE FIRED:**  \nAfter the first verified r7 replay showed **0 candidates / 4 legitimate ML reviews / known methodology conflicts**, the session should have been closed with a footer and all further totals research moved to a new bounded session."]}, "create_time": 1790316319.246934, "end_turn": true, "id": "82cdadeb-d6b0-4545-9411-6edc07b5d2fc", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [], "conversation_followup_suggestions_eligible": true, "cot_version": "v5", "default_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "finish_details": {"stop_tokens": [200002], "type": "stop"}, "gizmo_id": "g-p-6ab18502d1248191852c67e6b66490c0", "is_complete": true, "message_type": "next", "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "model_switcher_deny": [], "parent_id": "727b4acb-e9ce-416f-bd44-f4c01c3d5804", "request_id": "5d0a3ffe-24cd-4fa4-92ea-b7863b63cfc2", "resolved_model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "search_result_groups": [], "story_events": [], "thinking_effort": "standard", "turn_exchange_id": "bd242ce1-d8f0-461e-a0d3-9431d2c1b5dd", "working_turn_id": "bd242ce1-d8f0-461e-a0d3-9431d2c1b5dd"}, "recipient": "all", "status": "finished_successfully", "update_time": 1790316921.760779, "weight": 1}], "moderation_results": [], "owner": null, "page_info": {"end_cursor": "82cdadeb-d6b0-45